# TODO - CUDA superoptimizer

## Objective

Implement GPU-parallel candidate synthesis, pruning, equivalence testing, cost
evaluation, and verified block stitching.

## Acceptance

- GPU candidate generation/evaluation can be disabled without changing
  correctness, and every returned winning block is revalidated by the trusted
  verifier before catalog or compiler admission.
- A CPU/reference path remains sufficient for correctness, and accelerator
  failure/unavailability changes performance rather than semantic acceptance.
- Performance conclusions use equivalent workloads and report raw-sample
  provenance, resource budgets, dispersion/uncertainty, and failure/success
  behavior rather than only a best-case number.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.
- Consume the same versioned semantic candidate batch, exact pruning/cost
  rules and independent verifier used by the CPU optimizer. GPU-friendly
  packed state and resident batching must be evidenced; copying one complete
  15-trit VM memory image for every tiny candidate is not an assumed scalable
  strategy. CUDA does not depend on stochastic/learned search completion.
- Prove live NVIDIA hardware acceleration on a predeclared representative
  large-candidate workload: compare >= 3 growing batch/difficulty points and
  >= 15 paired complete CPU/CUDA samples with transfer, preparation, memory,
  verification and total time separated. At least one large-workload end-to-end
  median must be >= 1.25x faster on CUDA with matched verification and no
  omitted overhead; otherwise the P8 scaling gate remains open. Do not invent
  CUDA availability or extrapolate from primitive-kernel microbenchmarks.


## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `accelerator/`, `algorithms/`,
  `optimizer/`, `benchmarks/accelerator/`, `tests/optimizer/`.
- Required evidence: CPU/reference differential results, device/resource
  metadata, failure/fallback tests, and benchmark samples for claimed speedups.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: `cuda-exact-vm-adapter`,
  `search-pruning-and-state-canonicalization`, `translation-validation`,
  `resumable-compilation-progress-sidecars`. Stochastic/learned search remains
  an optional proposal policy, never a prerequisite of CUDA evaluation.
- Performance evidence pending: raw measurements plus a reproducible
  scaling/statistical summary tied to exact workload and hardware/software
  identity.
